  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Assessing Bornean Orangutan (Pongo pygmaeus ssp. wurmbii) Population in Muara Kendawanangan Nature Reserve, Indonesia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The West Kalimantan province in Indonesia has been known as one of the principal habitats of the Critically Endangered Bornean orangutan. Illegal hunting, trade and continuing forest loss and conversion for plantations, exacerbated by the tragedy of recent forest fires that devastated most of the forest in West Kalimantan, mean that there is an urgent need for updated information on the remaining Bornean orangutan populations in this area of their historic home range. This information is very important to guide conservation actions that can maintain the populations connectivity and keep these intact within the landscape range. This proposal focuses on a rapid assessment survey of orangutan in the 1,490km2 Muara Kendawangan Nature Reserve (Ketapang district, West Kalimantan province). This information will be used to inform patrols of identified key areas and populations. The objectives are to: (1) Provide Muara Kendawangan Nature Reserve management access to robust, current information on habitat suitability and orangutan populations; and (2) Initiate monitoring of the orangutan populations using community patrols and by implementing an adaptive patrol management system.</narrative>
  </description>
